# High Availability & Scalability for EC2

AWS combines **vertical scaling, horizontal scaling, Auto Scaling, Load Balancing, and multiple Availability Zones** to build resilient applications.
- **Vertical Scaling** — make one EC2 instance more powerful by increasing CPU, RAM, or storage. Useful up to the hardware limit.
- **Horizontal Scaling** — add more EC2 instances instead of making one instance bigger.
- **Auto Scaling Group (ASG)** — automatically adds or removes EC2 instances based on demand.
- **Load Balancer** — distributes incoming traffic across multiple instances so no single server becomes overloaded.
- **High Availability (HA)** — run instances across multiple **Availability Zones (AZs)** so the application can keep running if an instance or an entire AZ fails.

### Typical Architecture
```text
                    Users
                      ↓
               Load Balancer
                /          \
             AZ 1           AZ 2
              ↓              ↓
           EC2 #1          EC2 #2
              \              /
               Auto Scaling Group
```

If traffic increases, the ASG can add more instances. If an instance or AZ fails, the Load Balancer sends trafic to the remaining healthy instances.

**Key idea**: scalability handles **increasing demand**, while HA keeps the application **available during failures**.